<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Go on William Collins</title><link>https://wcollins.io/tags/go/</link><description>Recent content in Go on William Collins</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en</language><copyright>© 2026 William Collins</copyright><lastBuildDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:25:13 -0400</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://wcollins.io/tags/go/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Checksums Don't Prove Who Built It</title><link>https://wcollins.io/posts/2026/rethinking-software-releases/</link><pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:25:13 -0400</pubDate><guid>https://wcollins.io/posts/2026/rethinking-software-releases/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;For a long time, my definition of a successful software release was pretty simple: the tests passed, the binaries built, and the checksums matched. Ship it! That still sounds reasonable, but there is a gap hiding between &lt;strong&gt;we built some bytes&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;users downloaded the bytes we intended to publish&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description><media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://wcollins.io/posts/2026/rethinking-software-releases/featured.png"/></item><item><title>gridctl</title><link>https://wcollins.io/projects/gridctl/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://wcollins.io/projects/gridctl/</guid><description/></item></channel></rss>